# Note Taker — example environment file.
#
# Copy it to `.env` in this directory if you want to override anything
# (`cp .env.example .env`, or `make bootstrap-env` — every `make db-*`/`make dev` target runs it for
# you); the app also boots from a bare clone with NO `.env` at all, which is the path `pnpm dev` takes.
# Never commit a real `.env` (.gitignore covers it; only this file is whitelisted).
#
# Deliberately short:
#   * The four `NOTE_ENCRYPTION_KEY*` variables are PR-06's, and the production section at the bottom is
#     PR-18's — the full list of what a deployed container reads, with its documented defaults commented
#     out so an operator uncommenting a line is making a decision rather than copying a template.
#   * There is intentionally NO `DATABASE_URL` line: leaving it unset is what makes one
#     `POSTGRES_PORT`/`POSTGRES_PASSWORD` edit move the dev container and every host process
#     together (`env.ts`'s `databaseUrl()` derives it). Writing a DSN here freezes it, because
#     `process.loadEnvFile` performs no `${VAR}` expansion.
#
# The readers of this file are Docker Compose (`--env-file .env`, both stacks), the host processes
# (`loadDevEnvFile()` in apps/server/src/env.ts), and `pnpm check:env` (PR-18), which judges a production
# `.env` as text against the rules in docs/DEPLOY.md §4. Same values, one edit.

# Selects which invariants `apps/server/src/env.ts` enforces at boot.
#
# This line is for Docker Compose's `--env-file` interpolation and for the human reading the
# file. NO Node process takes its mode from here: `loadDevEnvFile()` and `databaseUrl()` gate on
# the NODE_ENV already in the *process* environment, and every host script declares its own mode
# on its own command line (`NODE_ENV=development tsx …`, `NODE_ENV=test vitest …`). A stray file
# can therefore never turn a production boot into a development one, and `env.ts` still fails
# fast when the variable is genuinely absent — in every environment, with no default.
NODE_ENV=development

# ── dev Postgres: interpolated by docker/compose.dev.yml ──────────────────────────
#
# These four are the dev stack's whole contract. The container gets them as `POSTGRES_*`, and because
# `DATABASE_URL` stays unset, `databaseUrl()` composes the host DSN from the same four values on
# `localhost` — so one edit here moves the published port and every host tool at once.
#
# Dev defaults, not credentials: the dev container is published on 127.0.0.1 only (never the LAN), and the
# production stack ships its own values with its db unpublished (PR-18). Keep the password `[A-Za-z0-9]`
# (`openssl rand -hex 32`): it goes into the DSN raw, and `env.ts` rejects the reserved URL characters.
POSTGRES_USER=notetaker
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=notetaker
POSTGRES_DB=notetaker
# Host side of the dev mapping (`127.0.0.1:${POSTGRES_PORT:-5432}:5432`; the container listens on 5432).
# Dev-only: among the Compose files it appears in docker/compose.dev.yml alone (the prod db publishes
# nothing), and `databaseUrl()` on the host reads this same value.
POSTGRES_PORT=5432

# ── the app ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The single published port of the *production* stack (`"${HOST_PORT:-3000}:3000"`, PR-18); it is listed
# here so this file stays one coherent list. Nothing on the host reads it — `make dev` binds the API from
# HOST/PORT (env.ts, defaults 0.0.0.0:3000), and the dev Compose file never sees it.
HOST_PORT=3000

# ── note encryption (PR-06) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Every note body is AES-256-GCM encrypted at rest (ADR-0002). There is no plaintext mode: not in dev, not
# in test, not for an empty note. Leave all four variables alone for local work — with
# `NODE_ENV=development`/`test` and none of them set, the server generates one key on first boot and
# **persists** it to `.dev/note-key` (mode `0600`, directory created on demand, gitignored), so dev rows
# survive a restart and the fingerprint in the startup log is the same on every boot. Production never takes
# that path: `env.ts` refuses to boot without a key, because a production container that quietly encrypted
# with a key nobody configured is worse than one that will not start.
#
# Generate a key: `pnpm generate:key` (root `scripts/generate-keys.ts`), or the operator one-liner that PR-18
# documents for a production host. **Losing this key makes every note body unrecoverable** — the whole
# backup/restore drill is about this one value.
#
# NOTE_ENCRYPTION_KEY=           # base64 of exactly 32 bytes; anything else is a reported failure
#
# The mounted-secret form, so a file-based secret works without shell interpolation:
# NOTE_ENCRYPTION_KEY_FILE=      # e.g. /run/secrets/note_key (a trailing newline is fine)
#
# The rotation ladder. When the key is retired it moves *here*, still able to decrypt the rows it wrote, and
# stays here until no row carries its version any more — `rotate-note-key.ts` reports what is left (`--verify`).
# Removing an entry that rows still reference makes exactly those rows unreadable.
#
# QUOTING: single-quote the whole value. This file is sourced by a shell in the runbook and Compose's
# `--env-file` strips exactly one quote layer, so a bare `{"1": "…"}` is mangled before it arrives; the
# process wants the JSON object itself, braces and quotes intact.
#
# NOTE_ENCRYPTION_KEY_PREVIOUS=  # e.g. '{"1":"<base64 of the key being retired>"}'
#
# The version every new row is tagged with, and the version the rotation script rewrites rows *to*. Bump it
# by hand, in the same edit as the `_PREVIOUS` entry above: it is deliberately its own variable and never
# derived from that map, because deriving it as `max(previous)+1` collapses back to `1` the moment the map
# is emptied and silently orphans every row tagged 2.
NOTE_ENCRYPTION_KEY_VERSION=1


# ── production: the stack in docker/compose.yml (docs/DEPLOY.md) ─────────────────
#
# This section is documentation, not a second configuration: a production host builds its `.env` with the
# two commands in docs/DEPLOY.md §2 (a generated key, then the three `POSTGRES_*` values above) and needs
# nothing below this line to be uncommented. Everything here is listed for two reasons — the file stays one
# coherent reference for the §4 table, and `pnpm check:env` reads exactly these names, so an operator who
# *does* set one can see what it means and what it costs.
#
#   NODE_ENV      — never set it here. The runtime image bakes `ENV NODE_ENV=production` and compose.yml
#                   repeats it in the app service, so this line would be inert inside the stack and
#                   misleading in the sourced shell the runbook uses (`NODE_ENV=development` above is
#                   precisely the line that must not survive into a deployed file; `check:env` fails it).
#   DATABASE_URL  — never set it here either. compose.yml composes it from the `POSTGRES_*` trio with host
#                   `db` in its `environment:` block, which beats `env_file:`; `.env` carrying a DSN is a
#                   second, quietly different database (docs/DEPLOY.md §9). The one place the runbook sets it
#                   explicitly is the restore drill, as `docker compose run -e DATABASE_URL=…`.
#   PORT          — never set it here: compose.yml pins the container side to 3000 so `EXPOSE` and the
#                   image's healthcheck stay true. HOST_PORT is the knob.
#
# HOST_PORT=3000                # the single published mapping, "${HOST_PORT:-3000}:3000": the one address
#                               # and port a deployment has (already listed in the HOST_PORT block above —
#                               # uncomment it here rather than adding a second line for it).
# HOST=0.0.0.0                  # bind address *inside* the container; the compose file never overrides it.
# SECURE_COOKIES=false          # true behind TLS; env.ts refuses false when TRUST_PROXY=true or
#                               # registration is open.
# TRUST_PROXY=false             # true when something else terminates TLS. That something must forward
#                               # `Host`, or `X-Forwarded-Host` and `X-Forwarded-Proto`, because PR-07
#                               # measures the CSRF Origin against the authority the client used.
# ALLOW_REGISTRATION=false      # opens POST /api/auth/register; /setup is the only other door, and it
#                               # closes behind its first owner.
# SESSION_TTL_DAYS=30           # <= 365 in production, on pain of not booting.
# DB_POOL_MAX=10                # postgres.js pool size.
# LOGIN_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=10       # failures per 15 minutes per user+IP.
# MAX_NOTE_BYTES=1048576        # transport body cap, and the note size cap stacked on it.
# LOG_LEVEL=info                # pino level: fatal | error | warn | info | debug | trace.
# APP_NAME=Note Taker           # SPA title.
